  "hints": [
    "the array being sorted wasn't mentioned by accident - the brute force never uses that fact. what could you do with a sorted array that you can't do with an unsorted one?",
    "if you had a pointer at the smallest number and one at the largest, and their sum was too big, which pointer would you move, and which direction?",
    "start left at index 0 and right at the last index. if the sum is too small, move left forward; if too big, move right backward",
    "this replaces the inner loop entirely - you scan the array once, not once per starting point"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "still using a nested loop or a hash map instead of using the sorted property",
    "moving the wrong pointer when the sum is too big vs too small",
    "returning the values instead of the indices"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a list `numbers` already sorted in ascending order, and a `target`.\n\n**Find two different numbers that add up to `target`, and return their indices.**\n\nThere's always exactly one valid pair. Unlike the original Two Sum, this time the array being sorted is information you're expected to use.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "2 <= numbers.length <= 3 * 10^4",
      "-1000 <= numbers[i] <= 1000",
      "numbers is sorted in non-decreasing order",
      "exactly one valid answer exists"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "the answer is the first and last element",
      "negative numbers or zero in the array",
      "the array has exactly two elements"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
